# Bill Gates

William Henry Gates III (born October 28, 1955) is an American businessman, investor, philanthropist, and software developer best known as the co-founder of Microsoft Corporation. Alongside his childhood friend Paul Allen, Gates played a pivotal role in launching the microcomputer revolution of the 1970s and 1980s by developing software for early personal computers. During his career at Microsoft, he held the positions of chairman, chief executive officer (CEO), president, and chief software architect, while also remaining the largest individual shareholder until May 2014. Under his leadership, Microsoft established a near-monopoly in the personal computer operating system market, most notably through the MS-DOS and Windows platforms, though the company's aggressive business tactics frequently led to antitrust litigation. Since transitioning away from day-to-day operations at Microsoft in 2008, Gates has focused heavily on philanthropic endeavors and climate technology investments. Through the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation, which he co-founded in 2000, he has directed billions of dollars toward global health initiatives, extreme poverty eradication, and educational expansion. Furthermore, he has founded and funded ventures such as TerraPower, an advanced nuclear reactor design company, and Breakthrough Energy, a coalition dedicated to accelerating climate change innovation.

## Early Life and Education

Bill Gates was born in Seattle, Washington, on October 28, 1955, to William H. Gates Sr., a prominent attorney, and Mary Maxwell Gates, who served on the national board of directors for the United Way. At the age of 13, he enrolled in the private Lakeside School, where he was exposed to early computing technology. The school used proceeds from a rummage sale to purchase computing time on a General Electric (GE) mainframe. Gates became fascinated by the machine and wrote his first computer program—a tic-tac-toe game—in the BASIC programming language.

During his time at Lakeside, Gates formed a close friendship with fellow student Paul Allen, bonding over their shared interest in computers. In 1973, Gates enrolled at Harvard College, where he pursued a pre-law major while taking rigorous mathematics and graduate-level computer science courses. At Harvard, he met Steve Ballmer, who would later succeed him as Microsoft's CEO. However, Gates left Harvard after two years, with the support of his parents, to pursue his entrepreneurial ambitions in the emerging software industry.

## Microsoft Corporation

Gates's tenure at Microsoft profoundly shaped the trajectory of the modern software industry, shifting the commercial focus from hardware manufacturing to software licensing.

### Founding and Early Years

In 1975, after reading an article in *Popular Electronics* about the Altair 8800 microcomputer, Gates and Allen contacted Micro Instrumentation and Telemetry Systems (MITS), the creator of the Altair, offering to demonstrate a BASIC interpreter for the platform. Following a successful simulation and demonstration, MITS agreed to distribute the software as Altair BASIC. Consequently, Gates and Allen founded Microsoft (initially stylized as "Micro-Soft") in Albuquerque, New Mexico.

### The IBM Partnership and MS-DOS

Microsoft's transformative breakthrough occurred in 1980 when IBM approached the company to supply the operating system for its upcoming IBM Personal Computer (IBM PC). Gates directed IBM to Digital Research, the makers of the CP/M operating system, but those negotiations failed. Microsoft subsequently proposed its own operating system, acquiring the rights to 86-DOS (Disk Operating System) from Seattle Computer Products, a local hardware manufacturer.

Microsoft adapted the software for the IBM PC and delivered it as PC DOS. Crucially, Gates retained the copyright and the right to license the software to other hardware manufacturers as MS-DOS. As the IBM PC architecture became the industry standard and spurred a massive market of hardware clones, Microsoft became the dominant provider of operating systems for the entire personal computer ecosystem.

### Windows and Antitrust Litigation

To provide a graphical user interface (GUI) on top of the text-based MS-DOS, Microsoft released the first retail version of Windows in 1985. The platform steadily evolved, achieving global dominance with the release of Windows 95. Microsoft further solidified its market position by developing the Microsoft Office suite of productivity applications, successfully bundling software in a way that marginalized competitors.

Microsoft's rapid growth and business practices drew the scrutiny of regulatory agencies. In 1998, the United States Department of Justice initiated *United States v. Microsoft Corp.*, a major antitrust lawsuit alleging that the company had maintained an illegal monopoly in the PC market by bundling its Internet Explorer web browser with the Windows operating system. Gates defended his company's practices vigorously, but the court ultimately ruled that Microsoft had committed monopolistic violations, leading to a settlement that restricted some of its business practices.

## Transition and Philanthropy

In January 2000, Gates stepped down as CEO of Microsoft, transitioning the role to Steve Ballmer, though he remained chairman and created the position of chief software architect for himself. This shift allowed him to dedicate more time to the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation, a charitable organization established by merging his previous philanthropic family foundations.

In 2008, Gates transitioned out of day-to-day operations at Microsoft to work full-time at the foundation. The organization is structured to address complex global issues, focusing heavily on infectious disease eradication (particularly polio and malaria), agricultural development, sanitation infrastructure in developing nations, and educational reform in the United States. In 2010, alongside investor Warren Buffett, Gates established The Giving Pledge, a campaign encouraging billionaires to commit the majority of their wealth to philanthropic causes over their lifetimes.

## Post-Microsoft Ventures

Beyond his philanthropic work, Gates has remained active as an investor and founder, particularly in the energy sector, driven by his concern over global climate change.

In 2006, he founded TerraPower, a nuclear reactor design company focused on developing advanced, scalable nuclear technologies such as traveling-wave reactors and sodium-cooled fast reactors, aiming to provide safe and carbon-free energy. In 2015, he spearheaded the creation of Breakthrough Energy, an umbrella organization of investment funds and philanthropic initiatives designed to accelerate the development of sustainable energy technologies capable of reducing greenhouse gas emissions to net zero. He also manages his personal wealth through Cascade Investment, a private holding and investment company holding stakes in various sectors including hospitality, railways, and waste management.

